  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 940 AM Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...  Saturday will mark a transition to cloudy, much \ncooler, and wet conditions that will continue in cold air damming \nthrough the weekend.\n\nOn the nwrn periphery of a strong sub-tropical high over the swrn N. \nAtlantic, a positively-tilted srn stream trough/shear axis, and \nrelated convectively-amplified mid-level disturbances, will progress \nacross the OH Valley and Middle Atlantic today and the Southeast \nthis weekend. It will do so ahead of a broad nrn stream trough that \nwill amplify across ern Canada and the Great Lakes this weekend, \nwith related cyclone development likely from near James Bay into QC \nby 12Z Mon. In the tightening height gradient between the troughs \nand branches of the westerlies, the models depict the development of \na wswly to swly upr level jet streak from the lwr MS Valley to the \nMiddle Atlantic, though there is large model spread in its placement \nand orientation and related influence to vertical motion via both \nupr-level divergence and lwr-level convergence and Fgen within its \nentrance region. \n\nAt the surface, high pressure extending from near Bermuda to the \nSoutheast will be displaced by a wavy synoptic frontal zone, or a \ncouple of closely-spaced ones, forecast to settle swd and across \ncntl NC on Sat. The front(s) will likely stall and become quasi-\nstationary from SC to southeastern NC Saturday night, then perhaps \nretreat slightly nwwd across ern NC with the passage of a weak \nfrontal wave Sun into Sun night. A following, ~1028-1030 mb cP high \nwill progress across and offshore sern Canada this weekend, while \nextending swd across the Middle Atlantic and Carolinas. Though \nprogressive, the high will be favorably strong and located for cold \nair damming to develop over the srn Middle Atlantic and Carolinas. \n\nConvective allowing models depict an area of scattered to locally \nnumerous convection developing along the cold front from late Sat \nmorning through Sat evening over roughly the srn half of NC. Weak \nmid-level lapse rates (generally less than 5.25 C/km) will yield an \nonly weakly unstable environment with diurnal heating ahead of the \nfront, however. North of the front, patches of mixed character \nrain/showers will be possible amid moistening mid-levels and very \nweak elevated instability, atop developing and deepening nely flow \nwithin the building surface ridge and cold air damming. \n\nRain will become more-widespread, steadier, and increase in \nintensity Saturday night and Sunday, in a regime of strengthening \nisentropic upglide across the surface frontal zone and atop the CAD \nhigh. Although the models continue to vary in storm total rainfall \namounts and their placement, it remains likely that a relatively \nwidespread, soaking rain (with amounts generally between one and two \ninches) will result over cntl NC by the end of the weekend. Given \nthe likelihood of CAD, forecast high temperatures and diurnal ranges \nfor Sat night-Sun night have continued to trend lower. \n\nA reinforcing cold front and following cP/Canadian surface high will \nprogress across the srn Middle Atlantic early next week, with \nfollowing cool and dry fall conditions for cntl NC through at least \nmid week.",
